WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.200
Released almost 20 years ago, Jeepers&nbsp;
Creepers is a classic horror movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.680
and it’s even spawned two sequels. Like&nbsp;
any good scary film, it can still induce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:14.240
hair-raising fear years later, so here’s&nbsp;
a recap on what makes it one of a kind.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.080
Jeepers Creepers follows siblings Darius&nbsp;
"Darry" Jenner and Patricia "Trish" Jenner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.920
who begin the movie on a road trip home&nbsp;
from college. They’re on spring break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.000
in rural Florida on a country highway,&nbsp;
with time on their hands and taking the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.160
scenic route to where they’re going,&nbsp;
so the scary-movie setup is perfect.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.760
Unfortunately for Darry and Trish,&nbsp;
they don’t take the quicker way home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.280
Which means they cross paths with&nbsp;
a mysterious, demonic creature.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.880
Before they know it, a rusty, ominous-looking&nbsp;
truck starts tailgating Darry and Trish.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.000
It’s a subtle nod to another ominous truck&nbsp;
that made its appearance in the 1971 Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.600
Spielberg-directed TV movie Duel. In that movie,&nbsp;
star Dennis Weaver was in the sights of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.240
unseen driver behind the wheel of&nbsp;
a massive, dangerous Peterbilt 281.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.720
In the case of Jeepers Creepers, the audience&nbsp;
finds out pretty quickly that the driver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.720
of this truck is a strange creature&nbsp;
called the Creeper . He’s not human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.080
and he can fly – fast – which we find out&nbsp;
after Trish starts questioning his movements.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.160
Wondering how someone can loot their car and&nbsp;
get back and forth to the church — where the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.240
Creeper keeps his victims — in such record&nbsp;
time, Trish eventually takes a guess:

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.600
"Maybe he can leap tall&nbsp;
buildings at a single bound."

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.320
Yes, the Creeper can fly. Unlike many movie&nbsp;
monsters though, the Creeper doesn’t just come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.520
out at night. He not only drives but also appears&nbsp;
to do his business during all hours of the day.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:40.080
Darry and Trish first see the Creeper&nbsp;
dumping bodies at the abandoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.960
church that's conveniently located in plain&nbsp;
view of the road. Not only do they see this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:49.680
strange figure unloading corpses into a&nbsp;
large pipe that sticks out of the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.240
but they get a clear, full frontal shot of&nbsp;
the Creeper after he spots them watching him.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.560
In most monster movies, the monster&nbsp;
doesn't usually fight on equal terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.840
Monsters stick to their strengths. Vampires, for&nbsp;
example, do their monster mashing at night — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:08.800
for obvious reasons, as sunlight is one of their&nbsp;
biggest weaknesses. However, the Creeper doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.520
have that limitation – he isn’t afraid to&nbsp;
roam free and wreak havoc during the daytime.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.480
After the Creeper sees Darry and Trish watching&nbsp;
him, he proceeds to get in his truck and chase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.760
after them. He rams into their car several times&nbsp;
until their vehicle goes off the side of the road,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.200
and then he speeds off into the&nbsp;
distance. At this point in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.760
the Creeper is only trying to scare the siblings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:36.240
so they could have presumably gone on their way&nbsp;
mostly unscathed. But Darry decides to be a hero.

00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:40.800
Darry convinces his sister to go back to the&nbsp;
church to see what the Creeper is throwing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:46.160
the pipe. After all, blood-stained sheets that are&nbsp;
shaped like human bodies could be anything, right?

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.200
"Wrapped in a sheet."

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:49.840
"Wrapped and roped in a sheet."

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.200
"Wrapped and roped in a&nbsp;
sheet with red stains on it."

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.400
Darry hears something coming from the pipe,&nbsp;
so he has his sister hold his feet as he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.960
tries to get a better view of what lurks&nbsp;
below. He sees rats and freaks out. Trish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.360
lets go of his legs and he falls into the&nbsp;
pipe, finding an underground tomb filled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:12.160
with bodies and one poor victim who's&nbsp;
still barely alive but on his way out.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:13.400
"I found our body."

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:14.720
"You what?"

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.360
"I found our -- AHH Jesus! Get off!"

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.640
It’s the Creeper's hideout, also referred to as&nbsp;
the House of Pain. Instead of helping Darry find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.920
a way out, Trish does as he orders and leaves him&nbsp;
to fend for himself so she can try to flag down a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.440
passing car. But against all odds, Darry makes&nbsp;
it out, their car starts, and they drive away.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.280
Even at this point, Darry and Trish could&nbsp;
probably have left this freaky town and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.360
called for help once they were out of harm's&nbsp;
way. But Darry, of course, wants justice for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:48.320
the lifeless victims he found, so they go to&nbsp;
a nearby diner to contact the authorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.200
This, unfortunately, gives the Creeper more time&nbsp;
to find out that someone discovered his lair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.680
with all signs pointing to Darry and Trish.&nbsp;
It also leaves the door open for the Creeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.280
to discover that Darry and Trish are&nbsp;
in possession of something he needs.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.120
After finding the bodies and&nbsp;
linking them to the Creeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.920
the siblings contact local authorities to&nbsp;
solve a case involving hundreds of dead people.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.320
"Stuck up on the walls, like some&nbsp;
psycho version of the Sistine Chapel!"

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:18.480
As they get in deeper, Darry and Trish&nbsp;
actually take pretty logical steps to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:21.520
deal with the monster. And that’s&nbsp;
saying something for a horror movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:26.080
a genre that routinely sees the characters&nbsp;
do the exact opposite of what they should do.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.160
Even after the first cops to respond are&nbsp;
killed by the Creeper, the siblings drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.400
to an old woman's house and ask to call&nbsp;
the police. Caught in the crosshairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.360
the Creeper kills the woman. Escaping to their&nbsp;
vehicle, Darry and Trish momentarily hurt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.920
Creeper by running him over. By now, they've&nbsp;
seen his inhuman face and know he can fly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.240
So it wouldn't have been out of the ordinary for&nbsp;
them to stop seeking help from the authorities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.640
And typical horror-movie logic dictates&nbsp;
here that Darry and Trish go rogue without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:56.320
consulting the police anymore, thinking&nbsp;
that no one else would believe their tale.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.080
"I'm being chased by a guy who likes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.160
pull tongues out of severed heads with his&nbsp;
teeth. Is there a special extension for that?"

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.840
But instead they doggedly press&nbsp;
on and go to a police station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.280
trying again to alert local law&nbsp;
enforcement. This inadvertently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.720
involves them in hearing about what&nbsp;
a lady sees in her psychic dreams.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.320
Darry and Trish first find out what the Creeper&nbsp;
is because of a random phone call. While they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.200
in the diner, an unknown woman calls them on a&nbsp;
payphone that happens to be right next to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.760
The woman somehow knows who they are and the&nbsp;
danger that lies ahead. She tells them that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.160
if they hear the song "Jeepers Creepers,"&nbsp;
they need to run away as fast as possible.

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:37.040
Only after they arrive at the police station&nbsp;
does the audience discover who the woman is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.200
a psychic named Jezelle Gay Hartman.&nbsp;
Jezelle tells them that the creature is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.280
ancient being that awakens every 23rd spring for&nbsp;
23 days to feast on the living. The Creeper eats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.080
human body parts that then become part of him,&nbsp;
finding his victims by smelling their fear:

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.480
“Somethin’ it can smell. Somethin’ that tells it&nbsp;
if there’s anything inside someone that it might…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:01.760
like.”

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:04.080
And he’s found something he&nbsp;
wants from Trish and Darry.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:10.800
The Creeper not only cannibalizes victims but is&nbsp;
also virtually impossible to kill. Case in point:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.760
Trish runs over the Creeper&nbsp;
multiple times with her car.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:14.720
"You think he's dead?"

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.840
"They never are."

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.000
The Creeper momentarily appears mutilated&nbsp;
and dead. But then a wing sprouts up from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.240
the Creeper and the siblings drive off in fear&nbsp;
— they have no clue what they're dealing with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.640
but they're not going to stick around to find out.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:37.360
The Creeper follows Trish and Darry to the&nbsp;
police station, and rejuvenates himself by eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.400
prisoners' body parts. So while the Creeper can be&nbsp;
hurt, at least in this first film in the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:47.040
he can't be killed. That makes Jeepers&nbsp;
Creepers even more terrifying, since the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:52.720
only way to defeat the Creeper is to survive his&nbsp;
23 days of terror. Darry never stood a chance.

00:06:53.836 --> 00:06:59.120
The movie takes its name from the 1938 song&nbsp;
"Jeepers Creepers." But the song itself isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:04.000
just the title of the movie - it also plays&nbsp;
a significant, sinister role in the story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:09.200
After Jezelle plays it for Darry and Trish on the&nbsp;
phone, she gives them an ominous warning to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:14.480
whenever it's played. They hear it again in their&nbsp;
car. Then Jezelle sings it at the police station.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:22.320
"Jeepers Creepers, where'd ya get those peepers?"
 
Perhaps most maddening of all, the song plays at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.600
the end of the movie during the&nbsp;
big reveal. Since the Creeper is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:30.080
attracted to specific humans and will stop&nbsp;
at nothing until he gets what he desires,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.720
the song's lyrics are decidedly sadistic&nbsp;
when paired with Jeepers Creepers the movie.

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.200
The lyrics, in fact, foreshadow Darry's fate.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.120
Jeepers Creepers has a memorable ending,&nbsp;
easily forgotten over time. And, unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:48.880
only one of the two main characters survive.&nbsp;
Trish, in a last-ditch effort to save her brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.920
tries sacrificing herself, telling the Creeper&nbsp;
to take her instead because she's stronger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.120
The Creeper contemplates her offer but then flies&nbsp;
off into the night with Darry. Trish lives on to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.680
tell her story, but Darry perishes at the&nbsp;
end of Jeepers Creepers in a brutal death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:08.320
Darry isn't shown again until the graphic ending&nbsp;
in which his eyes are missing. The Creeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:12.400
as if he's posing for the camera, stands on&nbsp;
the other side of Darry and looks through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.200
the holes in Darry's face. The Creeper's eyes&nbsp;
have presumably been replaced with Darry's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:21.360
disturbingly making Darry's eyes appear to&nbsp;
look through the holes of where they once were.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.760
Not only does the Creeper survive&nbsp;
the first installment – it ends on an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.160
outright sinister note, with no hope in sight.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:32.240
A defeated and emotionally drained Trish&nbsp;
leaves the police station not knowing if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.800
she'll ever see her brother again. It's fair&nbsp;
to assume that she knows she's already lost him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.440
as there's no way of finding the Creeper — and&nbsp;
he can't be killed. And, of course, the final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:46.400
scene of the movie features the Creeper still&nbsp;
alive and peacefully working in his new lair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:50.880
now with Darry's eyes in his own head. It's&nbsp;
no wonder Jeepers Creepers haunts people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.840
dreams to this day. This isn’t a movie you forget&nbsp;
because of how long ago it came out — more like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.360
movie you forget because the ending is the stuff&nbsp;
of nightmares and you choose not to remember.

